the program China's top legislative body  has unanimously approved a national  security law for the territory that may  crush political freedoms it's the most  far-reaching development in Hong Kong's  history ever since it was handed over to  China in 1997 acts of subversion could  now face a maximum sentence of life in  jail  critics say it'll be used to stamp out  all forms of dissent and it comes after  months of mass unrest sparked by fears  of China's tightening grip on Hong Kong  the legislation aims to cement Beijing's  control over the semi-autonomous  territory and reshape the future of one  of the world's major financial centres  Adrian Brown is standing by for us with  reaction out of Hong Kong first let's  cross over to Beijing where Katrina u is  waited for us in Katrina really just  talk us through the process of what we  know about how this law and legislation  has been discussed in Beijing we know  that China's Standing Committee its top  legislative body met at 9:00 a.m. today  on Tuesday to discuss this and shortly  after to vote on this soren shortly  after it was passed unanimously now only  one member of that Stanley committee is  a is from Hong Kong but they did have 12  observers from Hong Kong during the  discussion of this law which has taken  place since Sunday now you know it's a  hefty law it consists of six chapters  with 66 different articles underneath  that and what is most concerning some  legal experts is that state media here  have flagged the fact that Beijing will  have jurisdiction over very few of those  crimes meeting that extradition to the  mainland will be possible also we know  that life imprisonment could be one of  the heavier or the heaviest penalties  for breaking any of these crimes  mentioned in this law we're still still  waiting on more details we should have  that later on today but moving forward  we know there will be a national  security office set up in Hong Kong most  likely to be headed by somebody in  China's and Ministry of Public Security  somebody involved in intelligence and  there are a few more steps before the  small is actually implemented but  ultimately it will be inserted into Hong  Kong's Basic Law its mini Constitution  completely bypassing  Kong's own legislative process and we  have to remember that this has been a  tremendously fast-tracked process it's  been just about 40 days since this  national security law was first  announced during China's National  People's Congress its largest political  gathering earlier this year and usually  these laws takes years at least a year  for this to be passed and that really  does demonstrate Beijing's determination  to stamp out this pro-democracy protests  which many say are basically have been a  threat to Beijing's power in Hong Kong  for its part China's leaders say that  that is not the case this national  security law is about protecting Hong  Kong's stability Hong Kong's prosperity  this is all about a fresh new start for  Hong Kong but regardless we have to also  look finally at the timing of this  tomorrow is July the first the  anniversary of Hong Kong's handover from  Britain back to China and ultimately  Beijing seems to be sending a very  strong message and that is that in Hong  Kong  it is Beijing that is in charge Catrina  you in Beijing thank you well let's get  reaction now from Hong Kong Adrienne  Brown is our correspondent standing by  for us and since we heard the news just  a few hours ago Adrienne we've been  wondering what the reaction would be  from Hong Kong we're now getting more  voices speaking pro-democracy leaders  now speaking about their future  intentions what more do we know yes  that's absolutely right even before  local media announced that this law had  been passed it was already beginning to  have a chilling effect now Joshua Wong a  well-known young democracy activist has  said that he's going to be resigning  from the political party he helped to  set up  he believes he'll be a target of this  new security law and he says he's  leaving the party in order to practice  his own beliefs privately also another  member of the same party Nathan law has  also announced his resignation and a  smaller pro-democracy party called the  Hong Kong National Front has also  announced its disbanding so you can see  the effect already that the law is  starting to have and I think actually  that will please China's leaders because  this  or more than anything else I think is  there as a warning  now China has said in the past this law  was coming it had warned that certain  red lines had been crossed it had said  there would be consequences now we're  seeing those consequences in action  we've also been hearing from Carolyn the  embattled chief executive of Hong Kong  who his contree nough mentioned has been  completely bypassed in this process  along with her legislature but she  dodged questions about the new law at  her Tuesday morning media conference she  said she won't comment until the law has  actually been confirmed as having been  passed and we expect to have that  confirmation later this afternoon as  well as confirmed details of exactly  what this law contains but according to  state media during the past few weeks  this law will allow mainland agents to  operate legally on the ground here in  Hong Kong where a new security body is  also going to be established so this  without dales without doubt sohail is  the most profound impact the most  profound decision on Hong Kong since the  handover from Britain to China almost 23  years ago of course it is a developing  story we'll check in with you later  with that Adrienne Brown there in Hong  Kong thank you well while China's top  legislature has kept the draft of the  law under wraps some details have  emerged over the past few weeks so let's  take a look at those the law  criminalizes acts of secession  subversion and terrorism and collusion  with foreign forces and as we have been  saying it'll also allow members of the  Chinese security services to set up and  operate an agency in Hong Kong Beijing  will get jurisdiction over some national  security cases related to the city  finally it'll give Hong Kong's leader to  power at the power to choose judges that  will preside over cases while Tammy Tran  is a legislator and convener of the  pro-democracy camp in Hong Kong she  joins me now via Skype good to have you  with us on the program it does seem that  China has circumvented the legislature  in Hong Kong  how significant is that in the way that  china now deals with Hong Kong in the  future well by passing this law I would  say this is like a death certificate of  one country two systems' back in the 90s  the Chinese government had promised not  only to Hong Kong people but to the  world that Hong Kong will implement one  country two systems that means we can  keep our own judiciary the legislature  as well as the own administration but  now you can see that by passing this law  within like 40 days without any  consultation in Hong Kong without  disclosing the details of this  legislation  there's definitely bypassing the  administration as well as the  legislation in Hong Kong and the details  that you have mentioned of course it's  not confirmed yet but you can see that  not only the legislature and the  administration is under pressure you can  see also our judicial independence is  also also under very great pressure in a  sense that it may affect the Hong Kong  judiciary in the long run do you think  that you will have any useful part in  Hong Kong's future in the weeks months  and years ahead or are you now obsolete  well it's really very much depends on  whether we can whether the Chinese  government still respect the legislature  because in Hong Kong we can all we  always have freedom of speech we can  always have a freedom of expression but  under this legislation which likely will  be promulgated tomorrow are all our  freedom and rights are still protected  under our basic law or whether this law  will come about or our Hong Kong local  legislature as well as the basic law so  that means our future is very uncertain  although some people said that this  legislature would only affect a small  number of people but you can see that  under the definition of national  security in China it covers a very very  wide range of aspects for example I  economies education as well as religion  yeah so I really don't know what's what  will happen in even tomorrow so it's  very much depends on whether our  administration will exercise  self-restraint mr. Trump very briefly  then I want to ask you about your  reaction to news at Joshua Wong and  Nathan law are stepping down from you  might say the high profile role they've  played in pro in the pro-democracy  movement in Hong Kong what's your  reaction to that and do you expect the  international community to take you may  say the mantle of criticism of the way  Beijing have handled this to protect the  likes of Joshua Wong and Nathan law well  by passing this law within such a short  period of time I do believe that the  Chinese government have this very strong  determination not only to send a warning  but probably will take action that's of  course I'm a bit pessimistic but I I  totally of course respect the decision  of Nathan as well as Joshua but I  understand that they are under great  pressure but at the same time I think  they they would continue to fight for  democracy in their own ways but I do  believe that the impact will will will  be will be more so we can we may we may  see some more impact on not even the  younger generations but also some other  political parties or organization in in  the near future okay we shall see what  happens to Nia chan joining us from Hong  Kong thank you very much for your time  thank you

